   "text": "# The Press That Remade the World: A History of Printing and Its Consequences\n\nFew inventions in human history have exerted as profound and lasting an influence on the course of civilization as the printing press. The ability to reproduce text mechanically\u2014rapidly, cheaply, and in quantity\u2014transformed the way knowledge was stored, transmitted, and contested. It reshaped religion, politics, science, commerce, and culture across every continent it reached. To trace the history of printing is to trace the history of ideas themselves: how they spread, who controlled them, and how they toppled established orders. This essay examines the long prehistory of printing, the revolutionary innovations of the fifteenth century, the spread and regulation of the press, its role in the great upheavals of the early modern period, its industrialization, and its complex legacy in the modern world.\n\n## Precursors: The Deep Roots of Reproduction\n\nLong before movable type appeared in Europe, societies around the world developed sophisticated methods for reproducing text and images. The impulse to multiply the written word is ancient. Mesopotamian scribes pressed cylinder seals into clay; Romans stamped coins and carved inscriptions in stone; and Chinese artisans produced stamped seals and rubbings from carved stone steles centuries before the Common Era. These techniques demonstrated",
