   "text": "# The History of the Printing Press: From Woodblock to Revolution\n\nThe history of the printing press is, in many ways, the history of how knowledge became public. Before mechanical printing, the written word was the preserve of a small elite\u2014scribes, clerics, and scholars who labored for months over a single manuscript. The invention of movable type in the middle of the fifteenth century shattered that monopoly and set in motion a chain of transformations that would reshape religion, science, politics, and culture across the globe. To trace the story of the printing press is to trace the story of modernity itself, with all its promise and its perils.\n\n## Precursors: Woodblock Printing in East Asia\n\nLong before Gutenberg, printing existed in Asia. In China, woodblock printing\u2014carving a page of text or an image in relief on a wooden block, inking it, and pressing paper against it\u2014was well established by the Tang dynasty. The earliest surviving dated printed book, a copy of the Diamond Sutra from 868 CE, was produced this way, and it was discovered in the caves at Dunhuang. The technique allowed Buddhist monasteries to reproduce sutras in large numbers, earning merit through mass dissemination of scripture. By the Song dynasty, wood",
